{"abstract":"The influence of bias in datasets on the fairness of model predictions is a topic of ongoing research in various fields. We evaluate the performance of skin lesion classification using ResNet-based CNNs, focusing on patient sex variations in training data and three different learning strategies. We present a linear programming method for generating datasets with varying patient sex and class labels, taking into account the correlations between these variables. We evaluated the model performance using three different learning strategies: a single-task model, a reinforcing multi-task model, and an adversarial learning scheme. Our observations include: 1) sex-specific training data yields better results, 2) single-task models exhibit sex bias, 3) the reinforcement approach does not remove sex bias, 4) the adversarial model eliminates sex bias in cases involving only female patients, and 5) datasets that include male patients enhance model performance for the male subgroup, even when female patients are the majority. To generalise these findings, in future research, we will examine more demographic attributes, like age, and other possibly confounding factors, such as skin colour and artefacts in the skin lesions. We make all code available on Github.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Raumanns, Ralf","Schouten, Gerard","Pluim, Josien P.W.","Cheplygina, Veronika"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-776439"],"filters":{"added":"2025-01","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Eindhoven University of Technology","Fontys University of Applied Sciences"],"keyword":["Adversarial learning","Bias","Fairness","Multi task learning","Skin lesions"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dataset distribution impacts model fairness single vs multi task learning","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-SA","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.17543","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-776439","ids":["itu-ad7aea0e-1b0f-4a0a-8778-01104bc66c8a"],"keyword":["Adversarial learning","Bias","Fairness","Multi-task learning","Skin lesions"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-3BD1B5588B8E464C7F7CC8D4A7790797"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-SA","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2407.17543","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"nl","key":"fontys university of applied sciences","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Fontys University of Applied Sciences","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"nl","key":"eindhoven university of technology","loc":["Netherlands","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Eindhoven University of Technology","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Data-intensive Systems and Applications","IT University of Copenhagen, Data, Systems, and Robotics, Pattern Recognition Revisited lab"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ralf","key":"raumannspau","last":"Raumanns","name":"Raumanns, Ralf","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Gerard","key":"schoutenpau","last":"Schouten","name":"Schouten, Gerard","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Josien P.W.","key":"pluimpau","last":"Pluim","name":"Pluim, Josien P.W.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Veronika","key":"cheplyginapau","last":"Cheplygina","locid":"1c4bfd79-4336-4b17-a672-9acb465f15c0","name":"Cheplygina, Veronika","orcid":"0000-0003-0176-9324","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"edition":"15198","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"MICCAI Workshop on Fairness of AI in Medical Imaging (MICCAI FAIMI)","volume":"LNCS"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ad7aea0e-1b0f-4a0a-8778-01104bc66c8a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Dataset Distribution Impacts Model Fairness: Single Vs. Multi-task Learning","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-776439","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"We present ENHANCE, an open dataset with multiple annotations to complement the existing ISIC and PH2 skin lesion classification datasets. This dataset contains annotations of visual ABC (asymmetry, border, color) features from non-expert annotation sources: undergraduate students, crowd workers from Amazon MTurk and classic image processing algorithms. In this paper we first analyze the correlations between the annotations and the diagnostic label of the lesion, as well as study the agreement between different annotation sources. Overall we find weak correlations of non-expert annotations with the diagnostic label, and low agreement between different annotation sources. Next we study multi-task learning (MTL) with the annotations as additional labels, and show that non-expert annotations improve the diagnostic performance of (ensembles of) state-of-the-art convolutional neural networks. We hope that our data","author_count":5,"contributors":["Raumanns, Ralf","Schouten, Gerard","Joosten, Max","Pluim, Josien P.W.","Cheplygina, Veronika"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384395"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Netherlands"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Eindhoven University of Technology","Fontys University of Applied Sciences"],"keyword":["Crowdsourcing","Ensembles","Multi task learning","Open data","Overfitting","Skin cancer"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"enhance enriching health data by annotations of crowd and experts a case study for skin lesion classification","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.59275/j.melba.2021-geb9","version":"Published Version"},